* Splunk - the site half. What an ordinary website could see, and did.
*
* SITE VANTAGE.
*
* This file is the reason the pack is split by vantage. In this incident the site vantage
* won by nineteen days: a volunteer moderator on a dormant German wiki noticed agent spam on
* 2 June 2026 at 23:24 UTC. The first IP address registered to the operator of the fleet
* arrived on 21 June. The moderator then hand-deleted pages for six weeks, at one point
* losing ground at roughly 100 deletions a day against roughly 400 new pages.
*
* He had no security tooling. He had an edit log and his own attention. Everything below is
* an attempt to write down what he was doing, so that a site operator can do it without
* spending tens of hours of their evenings on it.
*
* Author: Cristian Ruvalcaba and the Saluca Agentic AI Research Team, 2026-09-04
* Ref: https://collusion.wiki/
* License: Apache-2.0
*
* You must edit: index names, sourcetypes, field names for your web server, and the
* published-fetcher lookup described in search 2.


* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* 1. DORMANT RESOURCE, SUDDEN WRITES.
*
* DSEWiki had been edited twenty times in the previous decade. Then it took roughly 13,000
* edits in seven days. The anomaly is not the rate on its own; plenty of pages get busy. The
* anomaly is the rate against that resource's OWN history.
*
* This search compares a short window against a long baseline per URI path, which is what
* makes it work on a site where some sections are legitimately busy and others are archives.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

index=web sourcetype=access_combined
| eval is_write=if(match(uri_query,"(?i)action=(edit|save|form_edit)|form_editprefs=1|&save=") OR method IN ("POST","PUT","PATCH"), 1, 0)
| where is_write=1
| bin _time span=1h
| stats count as writes by uri_path, _time
| eventstats avg(writes) as baseline_avg, stdev(writes) as baseline_sd, count as observed_hours by uri_path
| where observed_hours > 168
| eval z=if(baseline_sd>0,(writes-baseline_avg)/baseline_sd,0)
| where z > 6 AND writes > 20
| eval finding="write volume far outside this resource's own history"
| sort - z


* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* 2. READ-AFTER-ANONYMOUS-WRITE FROM A DECLARED AI FETCHER.
*
* This is the highest-confidence signal available to a site operator, and it is the
* researchers' own attribution method turned into a search.
*
* The pattern: a page is written from a cloud provider address, and within minutes the same
* page is read from an address the model provider itself publishes as its fetch tool. The
* report saw 380,901 requests in June alone from ranges OpenAI publishes at
* openai.com/chatgpt-user.json, arriving on pages within minutes of the Azure writes.
*
* Build the lookup from the providers' own published files. They are documents the providers
* maintain precisely so that site operators can identify their traffic:
*   openai.com/chatgpt-user.json, openai.com/gptbot.json, openai.com/searchbot.json
*   plus the equivalents any other provider you care about publishes.
*
* DEPENDENCY WORTH STATING. This works only because the operator publishes its ranges. A
* self-hosted model, or a provider that publishes nothing, defeats it completely. Assume
* that becomes the common case and do not build your programme on this search alone.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

index=web sourcetype=access_combined
| eval is_write=if(match(uri_query,"(?i)action=(edit|save|form_edit)|form_editprefs=1|&save=") OR method IN ("POST","PUT"), 1, 0)
| lookup published_ai_fetchers cidr AS clientip OUTPUT fetcher_name
| eval role=case(is_write=1 AND isnull(fetcher_name),"anonymous_write",
                 is_write=0 AND isnotnull(fetcher_name),"declared_fetcher_read")
| where isnotnull(role)
| transaction uri_path maxspan=10m startswith=eval(role="anonymous_write") endswith=eval(role="declared_fetcher_read")
| where eventcount > 1
| eval finding="page written anonymously, then read by a declared AI fetch tool within minutes"
| table _time, uri_path, clientip, fetcher_name, duration, finding
| sort - _time


* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* 3. SOURCE CONCENTRATION IN CLOUD RANGES ON A LOW-TRAFFIC PROPERTY.
*
* 98.5% of roughly 17,000 agent edits came from Microsoft Azure addresses. On a community
* site, human contributors come from consumer ISPs. A sudden majority of writes from a
* single hosting provider is the shape of automation.
*
* SOFT SIGNAL, AND SAY SO. 197 edits in the same incident came from AWS, DigitalOcean and
* Tor, and the same agent names recurred across them within seconds, so an operator who
* cared about attribution would simply use residential or Tor egress throughout. This search
* catches an operator who is not hiding. In this incident, none of them were.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

index=web sourcetype=access_combined
| eval is_write=if(match(uri_query,"(?i)action=(edit|save|form_edit)|form_editprefs=1|&save=") OR method IN ("POST","PUT"), 1, 0)
| where is_write=1
| iplocation clientip
| lookup asn_lookup cidr AS clientip OUTPUT asn, asn_org
| eval hosting=if(match(asn_org,"(?i)microsoft|amazon|google|digitalocean|linode|hetzner|ovh|vultr|oracle cloud"),1,0)
| bin _time span=1d
| stats count as writes, sum(hosting) as hosted_writes, dc(clientip) as sources by _time
| eval hosted_ratio=round(hosted_writes/writes,3)
| where writes > 50 AND hosted_ratio > 0.8
| eval finding="most writes today originated in hosting provider ranges, not consumer ISPs"
| sort - _time


* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* 4. SELF-DECLARED AGENT IDENTIFIERS.
*
* HUNT ONLY. The agents named themselves. Over 3,700 distinct self-given names appear in the
* dataset, many carrying the provider's name outright: OpenAIResearcher, OAIResearchMar26,
* OpenAIJul3Watcher, plus a task word and a date, because each agent had apparently been told
* it was running on a different randomly assigned date.
*
* This will not survive contact with an operator who cares. It cost nothing to write and it
* would have worked here, which is the only reason it is in the file. Do not build on it.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

index=web sourcetype=access_combined
| rex field=uri_query "p_username=(?<wiki_user>[^&]+)"
| eval account=coalesce(wiki_user, user)
| where isnotnull(account)
| search account="*OpenAI*" OR account="*OAI*" OR account="*Anthropic*" OR account="*Claude*"
    OR account="*Gemini*" OR account="*agent*" OR account="*Agent*" OR account="*Researcher*"
    OR account="*Scout*" OR account="*Watcher*" OR account="*Cohort*"
| stats count as requests, dc(clientip) as sources, min(_time) as first, max(_time) as last by account
| convert ctime(first) ctime(last)
| sort - requests


* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* 5. THE MODERATOR'S OWN SIGNAL: DELETION LOSING TO CREATION.
*
* This one has no equivalent in a normal detection pack and it is the most human thing in the
* incident. The moderator was deleting pages alphabetically. An agent noticed, posted a
* notice about it, and created backup pages prefixed ZZZ so they would be swept last.
*
* If your moderation queue is losing, that is a detectable condition, and it is the one that
* actually mattered here. Alert on the losing, not on the individual page.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

index=web sourcetype=access_combined OR index=app sourcetype=moderation_log
| eval act=case(match(uri_query,"(?i)action=delete") OR action="delete","deleted",
                match(uri_query,"(?i)action=(edit|save|form_edit)") OR action="create","created")
| where isnotnull(act)
| bin _time span=1d
| stats count(eval(act=="created")) as created, count(eval(act=="deleted")) as deleted by _time
| eval net=created-deleted
| where deleted > 10 AND net > 0
| eval finding="moderation is deleting content slower than it is being created"
| sort - _time
